@meta
id: 07-12
title: Drill — high, middle, low
chapter: 07
type: drill
skills: screen-HML, line-end
par: 20
minutes: 4
reps: 10
vscode: three clicks and three End presses
open: index.ts

@brief
Thirteen exports on one screen. The top one, the middle one and the bottom
one have lost their semicolons. Put them back.

Nothing to work out. The dojo will hand you the file ten times.

@keys
| `H` `M` `L` | high, middle and low on the screen |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |

@hints
Four keys per line: the screen motion, `A`, the semicolon, `Esc`.
--
Go `H`, `M`, `L` in that order and do not look between them. The three keys
sit under your right hand and should end up costing no more thought than
`j` does.

@solution
    H A ; <Esc>  M A ; <Esc>  L A ; <Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Sixteen keystrokes.

The reason to drill these three specifically is that they are the motions
people forget they have. `gg`, `G` and `}` all get used daily because they
answer questions you ask out loud. `H` and `L` answer a question you ask
with your eyes, and so they stay unlearned unless you deliberately put them
in your fingers.

They also pair up later. Once you have operators, `L` stops being only a
way to move and starts being a way to say "to the bottom of the screen",
and that turns out to be how you deal with a wall of output.

